Change log — Desmond GRO/XTC → gmx_MMPBSA wx miniapp
====================================================

GUI 1.3.0 / engine 9.1.1 (2026-10-29)
-------------------------------------

Remote execution on servers without the shared Data mount

- ``remote_alkyone.py`` renamed to the neutral ``remote_run.py`` (it
  was never alkyone-specific). The generated submission scripts keep
  their historical names (``alkyone_remote.sh`` / ``alkyone_job.sh``)
  so existing work directories stay consistent.
- New *rsync* staging mode for servers that cannot mount the data
  directory (e.g. ``fgusrvvs03``): the local driver pushes the input
  files — or the whole prepared work directory for *Run an existing
  prepared directory* — to remote scratch with rsync before the job,
  then pulls the results and Slurm logs back afterwards. Scratch is
  removed on success and kept for inspection on failure. The original
  *mount* staging mode (the server reads the shared sshfs Data
  directory and copies results back onto it) is unchanged.
- New Advanced-tab fields: **Remote SSH user** (optional; empty uses
  the SSH config default, otherwise all ssh/rsync calls use
  ``user@host``) and **Remote staging** (checkbox enabling rsync
  staging). Both persist in settings and profiles.
- *Check environment* and the submission summary honour the SSH user
  and show the active staging mode.
- Example profile ``fgusrvvs03_profile.json`` (host ``fgusrvvs03``,
  user ``jakubik``, partition ``CPU_job_Q``, walltime ``48:00:00``,
  scratch ``/scratch/jakubik``, conda ``~/miniforge3/bin/conda``,
  rsync staging) — load via *File → Load profile*.

Engine 9.1.1

- Antechamber's mol2 reader mis-parses MOL2 files whose molecule-name
  line (the line after ``@<TRIPOS>MOLECULE``) is empty: it then reads
  zero bonds and aborts on apparent open valences. The engine now
  substitutes the destination file stem as the molecule name before
  writing normalized MOL2 files.
- Version assertions in ``tests/`` updated for engine 9.1.1 / GUI
  1.3.0. (``tests/test_v9_1_cap_state.py`` failures are pre-existing
  from v9_1 and unchanged by this release.)

Helper scripts synced from the working installation

- ``run_gmx_structure_route.sh``: gmx_MMPBSA route that builds Amber
  topologies directly from the prepared PDB plus Antechamber MOL2,
  bypassing the ParmEd Amber → GROMACS → Amber topology round-trip.
- ``alkyone_profile.json``: saved working profile for the alkyone
  server (mount staging).

GUI 1.2.0 (2026-10-22)
----------------------

Concurrent remote submissions

- Multiple remote (Slurm) workflows may run at the same time: the GUI now
  tracks a list of process records instead of a single process. A further
  remote submission is allowed whenever no local workflow occupies the
  machine; local workflows remain exclusive.
- Each process gets a sequence tag shown in the log header, status bar and
  finish messages; while two or more processes are active, streamed log
  lines are prefixed with their tag.
- Progress bar and stage labels follow the most recently started job.
- Every remote job's Slurm JobID is tracked per process; **Stop** cancels
  all active processes (`scancel` for each parsed JobID plus SIGTERM/
  SIGKILL of each local process group), and closing the window stops all.
- Result-summary auto-loading uses the work directory of the job that has
  just finished.

GUI 1.1.0 (2026-10-16)
----------------------

Remote Slurm execution (new module ``remote_alkyone.py``)

- New *Remote execution* checkbox on the Calculation tab
  (*Complete workflow* and *Run an existing prepared directory*).
- On submission the GUI writes two scripts into the work directory:
  a local driver (``alkyone_remote.sh``) that allocates a scratch
  directory on the server, uploads the workflow engine and the job
  script, and hands over SSH; and ``alkyone_job.sh`` running on the
  server which stages the inputs off the shared mount, submits the
  engine via ``sbatch``, streams the job log back live, copies all
  results back to the mounted work directory, and propagates the job's
  exit code. The scratch directory is removed on success and kept for
  inspection on failure.
- New Advanced-tab fields: **Remote SSH host**, **Remote Slurm
  partition**, **Remote Slurm walltime**, **Remote scratch base**,
  **Remote conda executable**; all persisted in settings and profiles.
  Defaults: host ``alkyone``, partition ``LocalQ``, walltime
  ``48:00:00``, scratch ``/scratch/roshi``, conda
  ``~/miniconda3/bin/conda``.
- *Check environment* probes the server over SSH when remote execution
  is active (``gmx``, ``sbatch``/``squeue``/``sacct``, and the remote
  conda environment's Python modules and executables).
- *Show command* displays the remote submission summary.
- Slurm header uses ``--ntasks=<MPI> --cpus-per-task=1`` so that
  OpenMPI/PRRTE (used by gmx_MMPBSA) sees one slot per MPI rank;
  ``--cpus-per-task=N`` with a single task previously triggered the
  "not enough slots" error.
- *Stop* additionally issues ``ssh <host> scancel <jobid>`` for remote
  workflows.

Helper scripts synced from the working installation

- ``app/steric_preflight.py`` 1.2.0: adds
  ``finalize_cap_repair_report()`` recording the authoritative
  topology-aware validation result in ``cap_clash_repair.json``.
- ``app/repair_synthetic_caps.py``: renamed determination-by-atom
  exclusion structure (``excluded_by_atom``) and new
  ``--defer-hard-clash-validation`` option for pipelines running an
  authoritative topology-aware validation immediately afterwards.

